      "text": "Here’s the information you requested for your review of **\"People We Meet on Vacation\"** by Emily Henry:\n\n1. **Publisher, Release Date, and Standalone Status:**\n   - **Publisher:** Berkley\n   - **Release Date:** 2021\n   - **Standalone Status:** Yes, this novel is a standalone work.\n\n2. **Premise and Main Characters:**\n   - The story revolves around **Poppy** and **Alex**, best friends who have taken an annual vacation together for a decade. The narrative explores the evolution of their friendship and examines a pivotal moment two years prior that led to their estrangement. It blends elements of contemporary romance with themes of friendship, self-discovery, and second chances.\n\n3. **Dual-Timeline Structure:**\n   - The chapters alternate between present-day events during a trip taken by Poppy and Alex, and flashbacks to their previous vacations over the past decade. This dual-timeline effectively reveals their evolving relationship, showing both joyous and painful moments that contribute to their current dynamic, enhancing the emotional stakes of the narrative.\n\n4. **Opening Chapter Excerpt:**\n   - Here is an excerpt from the opening chapter to give you insights into Poppy's narrative voice: \n     > \"y for an entire summer. He was going home to East Linfield, Ohio. I was going home to Palmetto, Florida. We pulled up a map and drew the straightest possible line between the two towns, and then we crossed it out and drew a much crookeder line, because the first one went nowhere interesting.\"\n     >\n     > \"Alex drove. Alex always drove. I did the playlists and the snacks and the terrible jokes. Somewhere in a gas-station parking lot in western North Carolina, at three in the morning, I discovered that he had been laughing politely at me for six hours and finally, finally, made him actually laugh. He has a specific laugh that he only lets out when he is genuinely amused, a short, quiet, surprised laugh that begins with a pulled-in breath. It is the single most rewarding sound in the world. I have been chasing it ever since.\"\n     >\n     > \"That was nine summers ago. This year, for the first time, we were not going to...\"\n\nThis excerpt captures Poppy's introspective and humorous tone, which could help you characterize her narrative voice in your review. Let me know if you need anything else!",
